<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vel' d'Hiv Roundup</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16–17 July <a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Rafle du Vel' d'Hiv</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vel' d'Hiv Roundup was a mass arrest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 16–17 July 1942, carried out by French police under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> government at German request. About 13,000 people, including some 4,000 children, were seized; many were held in the Vélodrome d'Hiver cycling stadium before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a>, and very few survived. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> officially acknowledged its responsibility in 1995.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vel-dhiv-roundup.jpg" alt="Vel' d'Hiv Roundup" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Djampa · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rafle_du_Vel_d%27Hiv_plaque.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Vélodrome d'Hiver was an indoor sports arena in the 15th arrondissement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, used for track cycling, boxing and political rallies. In 1939 Jewish refugees were interned there, and in 1940 foreign women. On 14 May 1941, in the green ticket roundup, 3,747 foreign Jewish men were arrested after being summoned by post. The roundup of 1942 was planned by René Bousquet, Louis Darquier de Pellepoix, Theodor Dannecker and Helmut Knochen. At a meeting on 2 July Bousquet raised no objection and secured a compromise under which the French police would arrest only foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. The historian Julian T. Jackson argues that the policy had indigenous roots and was not forced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> by the Germans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>From 4:00 a.m. on 16 July 1942, some 9,000 French police, in teams of three or four, searched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, helped by a few hundred volunteers of Jacques Doriot's Parti Populaire Français. Paris police records show 13,152 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> arrested, among them 5,802 women and 4,051 children. Some were warned by the Resistance or hidden by neighbours. The arrested could take only a blanket, a sweater, shoes and two shirts, and most families were split up.</p><p>Some were taken to the camp at Drancy; the rest went to the Vélodrome d'Hiver, where about 7,500 are established to have been held, in heat under a painted glass roof, with one water tap and blocked lavatories. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> brought food and water. Those who tried to escape were shot, and some took their own lives. After five days the prisoners were moved to Drancy, Pithiviers and Beaune-la-Rolande and then deported by rail, mainly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The roundup accounted for more than a quarter of the 42,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> in 1942, of whom 811 returned. Apart from six adolescents, none of the 3,900 children detained at the Vel d'Hiv and deported survived. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-laval">Pierre Laval</a>, executed on 15 October 1945, had asked the Germans on 2 September 1942 not to demand more Jews.</p><p>For decades the French government refused to apologise, holding that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> state was illegitimate; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-mitterrand">François Mitterrand</a> repeated this in September 1994. On 16 July 1995 President Jacques Chirac acknowledged the role of the French state, and in 2017 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-macron">Emmanuel Macron</a> admitted its responsibility. A monument by Walter Spitzer and Mario Azagury was inaugurated on 17 July 1994 on the quai de Grenelle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vel' d'Hiv roundup” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Vel' d'Hiv Roundup</b> of 16 and 17 July 1942, French police under René Bousquet and Jean Leguay arrested 13,152 Jews, including 4,051 children, whom the Gestapo had not asked for, and sent them through Drancy to Auschwitz.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vichy-france">Vichy France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
